{
  "dom_id": "step-q28199",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(3-x)(1-x)^{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(3-0)(1-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(3)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(3-x)(1-x)^{2} \\\\  & =(3-x)({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251970/assets/20ba88abb40a98b3d832402627568a1536abde65018e09cce9c89e184128132b.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
